			"date": "2023-01-08T15:55:59.000Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\"><a href=\"https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>zachleat</span></a></span> That reminds me that there's an entire office in the Department of Defense dedicated to working \"with\" filmmakers:</p><p><a href=\"https://www.defense.gov/News/Inside-DOD/Blog/article/2062735/how-why-the-dod-works-with-hollywood/\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\"><span class=\"invisible\">https://www.</span><span class=\"ellipsis\">defense.gov/News/Inside-DOD/Bl</span><span class=\"invisible\">og/article/2062735/how-why-the-dod-works-with-hollywood/</span></a></p><p>Basically, if you want access to any actual military resources (planes, uniforms, etc.), then the DoD gets authority over your film. This very much explains why most films featuring the U.S. military cast the services in an uncritical light.</p>",
